From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 44/45] arch/amd64 bound no cif-claim at all, so every client allocation returned -1

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

arch/x86 got a bump allocator when this lab found that x86 shipped no cif-claim
and the IEEE 1275 `claim` service fell through forth/system/ciface.fs's
`else 3drop -1`. arch/amd64 was never given one. It has no
/openprom/client-services block at all, so on the 64-bit firmware every client
allocation has always returned -1.

Measured before writing this, with the positive control in the same run:

    " cif-claim" find-method   x86: FOUND     amd64: MISSING
    " cif-release" find-method x86: FOUND     amd64: MISSING

Nothing had noticed because the client-program ladder was built on ppc and the
revived x86, and no 64-bit client had ever asked for memory.

THE WINDOW CANNOT BE COPIED FROM x86, which is the part worth reading. x86 sizes
its window as 8 MiB up to virt_to_phys(0) -- the bottom of the client's address
window, just below the RELOCATED firmware -- and both ends move with
virt_offset. arch/amd64 does not relocate at all (long mode ignores segment
bases, Spike 1), so virt_to_phys(0) is 0 and x86's formula yields an EMPTY
window: a claim allocator that refuses everything, which is what it replaced.
The limit is derived from sys_info's memory map instead -- the end of the usable
range containing the base -- which is the same map `reg` is published from and
is therefore right at any -m size.

The other divergence is the return value: x86 hands back phys_to_virt(base)
because a client there runs in the firmware's rebased segments; amd64 runs in
place, so the physical address IS the address the client uses.

They are NODE METHODS on /openprom/client-services, not global words. ppc and
sparc install the same two names there via NODE_METHODS and arch/x86 wraps its
pair in the identical find-device/device_end, so being invisible to a global
$find is correct here rather than a second instance of the patch-14 binding bug.
TODO §13.1 records that distinction, after this lab briefly got it wrong in the
other direction.

Control: renaming the bind to "cif-claim-CONTROL" makes the new
memory-available track fail naming the missing method. (Deleting the bind
instead does NOT work as a control -- the function goes unused, -Werror stops
the build, and the track SKIPs on a missing artifact having tested nothing.)

--- a/arch/amd64/openbios.c
+++ b/arch/amd64/openbios.c
@@ -90,6 +90,81 @@
 	PUSH(0x9FFFF);
 }
 
+/* ---- `claim` for amd64 (TODO 17.3) ----------------------------------
+ * arch/amd64 bound NO cif-claim and NO cif-release, so the IEEE 1275 `claim`
+ * service fell through forth/system/ciface.fs's `else 3drop -1` and every
+ * client allocation on the 64-bit firmware failed. arch/x86 got a bump
+ * allocator when this lab found the same hole there; amd64 never did, and it
+ * went unnoticed because nothing here had asked a 64-bit client to allocate.
+ * Measured before writing this: `" cif-claim" find-method` finds it on x86 and
+ * does NOT on amd64.
+ *
+ * THE WINDOW CANNOT BE COPIED FROM x86, and that is the interesting part.
+ * x86 sizes its window as 8 MiB up to virt_to_phys(0) -- the bottom of the
+ * client's address window, just below the RELOCATED firmware -- and both ends
+ * move with virt_offset. amd64 does not relocate at all (long mode ignores
+ * segment bases, Spike 1), so virt_to_phys(0) is 0 and that formula yields an
+ * empty window. The limit here is DERIVED from the memory map instead: the end
+ * of the usable range that contains the base, which is the same sys_info the
+ * `reg` above is published from and is therefore correct at any -m size.
+ */
+#define CLAIM_BASE_PHYS	0x800000ULL
+
+static unsigned long long claim_next = CLAIM_BASE_PHYS;
+
+/* The end of the usable range containing CLAIM_BASE_PHYS, or 0 if the base is
+ * not in any usable range -- which would mean a machine too small to allocate
+ * from, and 0 makes the window empty rather than unbounded. */
+static unsigned long long claim_limit( void )
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < sys_info.n_memranges; i++) {
+		unsigned long long base = sys_info.memrange[i].base;
+		unsigned long long end  = base + sys_info.memrange[i].size;
+
+		if (CLAIM_BASE_PHYS >= base && CLAIM_BASE_PHYS < end)
+			return end;
+	}
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/* ( virt size align -- baseaddr|-1 ) */
+static void
+ciface_claim_amd64( void )
+{
+	ucell align = POP(), size = POP(), virt = POP();
+	unsigned long long base, limit;
+
+	if (virt) {		/* caller named an address; honour it */
+		PUSH(virt);
+		return;
+	}
+	if (align < 0x1000)
+		align = 0x1000;
+	base = (claim_next + align - 1) & ~((unsigned long long)align - 1);
+	limit = claim_limit();
+	if (!size || base + size > limit || base + size < base) {
+		PUSH((ucell)-1);
+		return;
+	}
+	claim_next = base + size;
+	/* No phys_to_virt() here, unlike x86: this firmware runs in place, so the
+	 * physical address IS the address a client uses. */
+	PUSH((ucell)base);
+}
+
+/* ( virt size -- ). A bump allocator cannot free; releasing in LIFO order is
+ * the only case worth honouring, and clients exit immediately after. */
+static void
+ciface_release_amd64( void )
+{
+	ucell size = POP(), virt = POP();
+
+	if ((unsigned long long)virt + size == claim_next)
+		claim_next = virt;
+}
+
 /* Spike 2: ( -- n )  run the context-switch self-test and push its result. */
 static void ctx_switch_word( void )
 {
@@ -178,6 +253,18 @@
 	 * on a tree that is not built yet faults rather than returning 0. */
 	publish_memory_ranges(&sys_info);
 
+	/* TODO 17.3. Node METHODS on /openprom/client-services, not global words:
+	 * ppc and sparc install the same two names there via NODE_METHODS, and
+	 * arch/x86 wraps its pair in the identical find-device/device_end. Their
+	 * being invisible to a global $find is correct here, not a second instance
+	 * of the patch-14 binding bug -- TODO §13.1 records that distinction after
+	 * this lab briefly got it wrong in the other direction. */
+	push_str("/openprom/client-services");
+	fword("find-device");
+	bind_func("cif-claim", ciface_claim_amd64);
+	bind_func("cif-release", ciface_release_amd64);
+	device_end();
+
 	bind_func("platform-boot", boot );
 
